Q: Why do we create a network in Docker?

One line:
So containers can talk to each other by name (DNS), without hardcoded IPs, with isolation from other networks.

────────────────────────────────────────
Simple example

Containers:
- spring-app → Java app
- mysql → database
- redis → cache

Create network:
docker network create my-network

Run on that network:
docker run --network my-network --name mysql mysql:8
docker run --network my-network --name spring-app my-app

Spring connects with container name (not IP):
jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/mydb

Redis:
redis:6379

────────────────────────────────────────
Why do we use a Docker network?

1) Container-to-container communication
   App → DB / cache / other services on same network.

2) Service discovery (Docker embedded DNS)
   Use names: mysql, redis, spring-app
   IPs can change on restart; names stay stable.

3) Isolation
   Containers on network-A cannot freely reach network-B (by default).
   Better than putting everything on one flat network.

4) No hardcoded IPs
   Hardcoding 172.17.x.x breaks when containers recreate.

────────────────────────────────────────
Important interview nuance (default bridge vs user-defined)

| Network              | Resolve by container name? | Typical use        |
| -------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------ |
| default bridge       | No (legacy linking only)   | quick single runs  |
| user-defined bridge  | Yes (DNS by name)          | app + db + redis   |
| host                 | uses host network stack    | special cases      |
| none                 | no networking              | locked down        |
| overlay              | multi-host (Swarm)         | cluster networking |

That’s why we often run:
docker network create my-network
instead of relying only on the default bridge.

Compose note:
docker compose creates a project network automatically and services reach each other by service name.

────────────────────────────────────────
“Securely” — say carefully

Docker network gives isolation + private communication on that network.
It is NOT automatically encrypted end-to-end (that’s TLS / app security).
Better phrase: “isolated communication using service names.”

────────────────────────────────────────
Interview answer (best short)

“We create a Docker network so related containers can communicate using DNS names like mysql:3306 instead of fragile IPs, and stay isolated
from containers on other networks. User-defined bridge networks provide this name-based discovery; the default bridge does not do it well.”

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ 20-second version

“Docker network = private LAN for containers. Same network → talk by name. Different network → isolated. Avoids hardcoded IPs.”
